{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"abfe95ca-e883-4a0c-ae59-da37fa533cb8","status":"SUCCESS","response":"I have updated the workout generator and documented the changes in [ANSWER.md](file:///tmp/h-Ad3IOv/ANSWER.md).\n\n### Summary of What Was Changed and Why\n\n1. **Change in [`gen.py`](file:///tmp/h-Ad3IOv/gen.py):**\n   - In [`hr_target`](file:///tmp/h-Ad3IOv/gen.py#L10-L14), changed the lower bound field key from `\"value\"` to `\"start\"`:\n     ```python\n     # Before:\n     return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n\n     # After:\n     return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n     ```\n\n2. **Why:**\n   - When building workout steps in Intervals.icu (`workout_doc`), using `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}` results in Garmin FIT export treating the range as a zone-mapped target, which clamps each corridor to the watch's internal heart rate zones.\n   - Using `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}` triggers the export of an instant HR corridor with explicit lower and upper target bounds computed against the athlete's configured LTHR (172 bpm), preventing the watch from snapping to device zones.\n","duration_seconds":161.722603,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":137249,"output_tokens":1860,"thinking_tokens":0,"cache_read_tokens":518187,"total_tokens":139109}}}
